---
import Article from '../../layouts/Article.astro';
import Shot from '../../components/Shot.astro';
import Icon from '../../components/Icon.astro';
import { url } from '../../lib/url';

const line = [
  { id: 'T-100', name: 'Syrup tank' },
  { id: 'M-100', name: 'Blender' },
  { id: 'F-200', name: 'Filler' },
  { id: 'C-300', name: 'Capper' },
  { id: 'L-400', name: 'Labeller' },
  { id: 'V-500', name: 'Inspector' },
  { id: 'P-600', name: 'Case packer' },
  { id: 'R-700', name: 'Palletizer' },
];

const pillars = [
  { icon: 'boxes', title: 'A model', body: 'What the machines are, how they connect and what each one measures.' },
  { icon: 'activity', title: 'A pulse', body: "The model's numbers are the machine's numbers, right now." },
  { icon: 'history', title: 'A memory', body: 'History to compare against, targets to judge by, alarms that speak up.' },
];

const oee = [
  { label: 'Availability', value: '94.9%', tone: 'a' },
  { label: 'Performance', value: '90.8%', tone: 'p' },
  { label: 'Quality', value: '99.28%', tone: 'q' },
];

const downtime = [
  { reason: 'Changeover', minutes: 25 },
  { reason: 'Labeller jams', minutes: 21 },
  { reason: 'Capper torque', minutes: 12 },
  { reason: 'Preforms short', minutes: 9 },
  { reason: 'Other', minutes: 6 },
];
const downtimeTotal = downtime.reduce((sum, d) => sum + d.minutes, 0);
---

<Article
  title="Digital twins for production lines: Nexus"
  description="What a digital twin really is on the factory floor, and how to build one in Nexus: a live PET bottling line, from the first tag to the finished screen."
  eyebrow="Guide"
  headline="Your production line, alive in a browser tab"
  standfirst="A digital twin isn't a 3D rendering for the boardroom. It's a living copy of your line, wired to the real thing, that tells you what's happening, what just happened and what to look at next. Here's what that looks like on a bottling line, and how to build one in Nexus."
  ogImage="/og/digital-twin.png"
  ogImageAlt="The Line 1 PET bottling twin in Nexus: OEE tiles, a drawing of the line with live readouts, alarms and shift charts"
  ctaHeadline="See your line as a twin."
  ctaBody="Bring a line and its tag list, and we'll show you what its twin looks like in Nexus."
>
  <Shot
    slot="visual"
    id="line-twin"
    loop
    controls
    eager
    caption="Line 1, a PET bottling line, running live in Nexus. Click any machine and the panel on the right follows it."
  />

  <h2>Meet Line 1</h2>
  <p>
    Line 1 bottles soft drinks in Hall B. Syrup leaves tank T-100 and meets water in blender M-100. From there, every bottle runs the gauntlet:
    filled, capped, labelled, checked by a camera, packed into cases and stacked onto pallets by a robot arm.
  </p>

  <figure class="line-strip" aria-label="Line 1, machine by machine">
    <ol>
      {
        line.map((m) => (
          <li>
            <span class="tag-id">{m.id}</span>
            <span>{m.name}</span>
          </li>
        ))
      }
    </ol>
  </figure>

  <p>
    In the twin, all of that fits on one screen. Each machine carries a small readout with the number that matters most for it: the tank's
    level, the blender's pressure, the fill volume (499.6 mL, if you're wondering), the capper's torque, the inspector's rejects, cases
    packed and pallets built. A green tag says Running. When a machine is starved, blocked or faulted, its colour tells you before you've read a
    word.
  </p>
  <p>
    Across the top is the line's scorecard: OEE against target, availability, performance, quality, good bottles against the shift plan, and
    energy per thousand bottles. Under the drawing is the story of the shift: output by hour against plan, line speed against setpoint, and where
    the downtime went.
  </p>
  <p>
    Then there's the part everyone enjoys. <strong>Click the capper and the panel on the right becomes the capper</strong>: its rate, drive
    temperature, availability, and the last fifteen minutes of drive temperature. Click the palletizer and the panel follows. Nobody digs through
    a menu or remembers a tag name. You point at the machine you're curious about.
  </p>

  <h2>So what is a digital twin, really?</h2>
  <p>Take away the buzzwords and a digital twin is three things working together:</p>

  <div class="pillars">
    {
      pillars.map((p) => (
        <div class="pillar">
          <span class="card-icon">
            <Icon name={p.icon} size={20} />
          </span>
          <h3>{p.title}</h3>
          <p>{p.body}</p>
        </div>
      ))
    }
  </div>

  <p>
    A drawing without data is a poster. Data without a model is a spreadsheet. A twin is where the two meet, and that's what makes it useful:
    you can ask it questions. Are we on plan? Which machine is costing us the most time? Was the capper running hot before it started
    tripping?
  </p>
  <p class="pull">A twin doesn't need to look real. It needs every number on it to be real.</p>
  <p>
    Notice what isn't on that list: photorealism. The Line 1 twin is a clean, simple drawing, and it works because every number on it comes
    straight from the line controller.
  </p>

  <h2>Why so many twins never leave the slide deck</h2>
  <p>
    Most twin projects don't fail because of the drawing. They fail because of the plumbing. The data lives in a PLC, a historian, a
    spreadsheet of shift targets and a maintenance log. The twin is built by a separate team in a separate tool, opened in a special client on a
    few PCs, and quietly goes stale the first time the line changes.
  </p>
  <p>
    The fix is to build the twin where the data already lives: in the platform that talks to your PLCs, records your history and raises your
    alarms. Then the twin isn't a project. It's just another screen.
  </p>

  <h2>Building Line 1 in Nexus</h2>
  <p>Here's how a line like this comes together in Nexus, from the first tag to the finished screen.</p>

  <ol class="build">
    <li>
      <h3>Connect the line</h3>
      <p>
        Nexus speaks the protocols already on your floor: <a href={url('/connectivity/')}>Modbus, S7, EtherNet/IP, OPC UA and MQTT</a>. Point it
        at the line controller and every value you care about becomes a tag.
      </p>
      <p>
        No PLC yet? Start with the built-in simulator and build the whole twin before the line is commissioned. When the hardware arrives,
        switch the tags to the real device and everything you built keeps working.
      </p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <h3>Model each machine once</h3>
      <p>
        A filler, a capper and a labeller are all, at heart, a machine with a state, a rate, a drive and some counters. Describe that once as a <a
          href={url('/templates/')}>template</a
        >, with its tags, alarms and parameters, and create an instance for each machine on the line.
      </p>
      <p>
        Need a capper with an extra torque reading? Make a Capper template that extends Machine and adds just that. Improve the template and every
        machine on the line gets the improvement.
      </p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <h3>Turn signals into answers</h3>
      <p>
        Raw signals become the numbers on the scorecard through expression tags. Availability is run time over planned time. Performance is actual
        speed over ideal speed. Quality is good bottles over all bottles. Multiply the three and you have OEE:
      </p>
      <figure class="equation" aria-label="Availability 94.9% times performance 90.8% times quality 99.28% is about 85.6% OEE">
        {
          oee.map((term, i) => (
            <>
              {i > 0 && (
                <span class="op" aria-hidden="true">
                  ×
                </span>
              )}
              <span class:list={['term', term.tone]}>
                <small>{term.label}</small>
                <strong>{term.value}</strong>
              </span>
            </>
          ))
        }
        <span class="op" aria-hidden="true">≈</span>
        <span class="term result">
          <small>OEE</small>
          <strong>85.6%</strong>
        </span>
      </figure>
      <p>
        Energy per thousand bottles works the same way: the energy meter divided by the good-bottle count. Every derived number is a tag like any
        other, so you can trend it, alarm on it and report on it.
      </p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <h3>Draw the twin</h3>
      <p>
        Nexus views are built in the browser. Drop in a drawing of your line as the backdrop, then place symbols and readouts on top. Tanks,
        conveyors, fillers, cappers and case packers are already in the symbol library, ready to show running, stopped and fault. KPI tiles hold a
        value, a target and a progress bar. Bind each one to its tag and it comes alive.
      </p>
      <p>
        The click-a-machine trick is a view property. Clicking a machine sets which one is selected, and the panel on the right reads that
        machine's tags through the property. One panel serves every machine.
      </p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <h3>Give it a memory</h3>
      <p>
        The <a href={url('/history/')}>historian</a> records every tag value, so the twin can look back as easily as it looks at now. The drive
        temperature trend, line speed against setpoint, output by hour with OEE on a second axis, and the downtime breakdown are all drawn from
        that history. The same numbers go into shift reports that send themselves when the shift ends.
      </p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <h3>Teach it to speak up</h3>
      <p>
        A twin that only shows things still needs someone watching it. <a href={url('/alarms/')}>Alarm rules</a> do the watching: a capper torque
        fault, a labeller temperature drifting, a process value out of its band. Each alarm has a priority and can be acknowledged or shelved right
        from the twin. If nobody responds, it escalates to whoever is on call.
      </p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <h3>Let the assistant sketch the first draft</h3>
      <p>
        Describe the screen you want and the <a href={url('/ai-assistant/')}>AI assistant</a> builds a draft from your real tags. It's a quick
        way to get a first layout onto the canvas, and nothing reaches operators until you publish.
      </p>
    </li>
  </ol>

  <h2>What changes when the line has a twin</h2>
  <p>
    Look at the downtime panel in the video. This shift, the line has lost {downtimeTotal} minutes, and the twin already knows where they
    went:
  </p>

  <figure class="pareto">
    <ul aria-label={`Downtime this shift, ${downtimeTotal} minutes in total`}>
      {
        downtime.map((d) => (
          <li>
            <span class="reason">{d.reason}</span>
            <span class="bar" style={`--w: ${(d.minutes / downtime[0].minutes) * 100}%`} />
            <span class="mins">{d.minutes} min</span>
          </li>
        ))
      }
    </ul>
    <figcaption>Downtime this shift on Line 1, largest first</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <p>
    Nobody had to put that report together on Monday. It's on screen while the shift is still running, so the conversation about labeller jams
    can happen while the jams are still happening.
  </p>
  <p>A few things teams notice once a line has a twin:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Dips explain themselves.</strong> The 08:00 bar in the output chart sits below plan, and OEE dips with it. Open the line speed history
      for that hour and the stops behind it are right there.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Everyone sees the same line.</strong> The operator at the filler, the supervisor on a tablet and the maintenance lead at home see the
      same live numbers, in a browser. There's nothing to install.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Targets stop being abstract.</strong> Every tile shows its target, so "are we having a good shift?" is answered at a glance.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>New starters learn faster.</strong> The twin is a map of the line that explains itself. Click around and you learn what each machine
      does and what normal looks like.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Energy becomes a production number.</strong> Kilowatt-hours per thousand bottles, right next to OEE, turns energy into something the
      shift can act on.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Changes are cheap.</strong> A new machine is a new template instance and a new symbol on the drawing. The twin keeps up with the line,
      not the other way round.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Start with one line and one question</h2>
  <p>
    The best twins start small. Pick one line. Pick the question you most want answered ("where does our downtime go?" is a great first one).
    Connect the tags that answer it, draw the line, add a scorecard, and put it in front of the people who run the line. Then let them tell you what
    to add next.
  </p>
  <p>Line 1 started exactly that way. Now it's the screen everyone keeps open.</p>

  <p class="next">
    <a class="text-link" href={url('/how-it-works/')}>See how Nexus fits together <Icon name="arrow-right" size={16} /></a>
  </p>
</Article>

<style>
  /* The line, machine by machine */
  .line-strip ol {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 8px 0;
    margin: 0;
    padding: 0;
    list-style: none;
  }
  .line-strip li {
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 8px;
    margin: 0;
    color: var(--text);
    font-size: 0.875rem;
    font-weight: 500;
    line-height: 1.2;
  }
  .line-strip li + li {
    margin-top: 0;
  }
  .line-strip li > span:last-child {
    padding: 7px 12px 7px 0;
  }
  .line-strip li:not(:last-child)::after {
    content: '';
    width: 18px;
    height: 2px;
    margin-right: 10px;
    border-radius: 2px;
    background: var(--grad);
  }
  .line-strip .tag-id {
    padding: 4px 8px;
    border-radius: 6px;
    background: var(--tint);
    color: var(--primary);
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 0.75rem;
  }

  /* Model, pulse, memory */
  .pillars {
    display: grid;
    gap: 12px;
    margin-top: 24px;
  }
  @media (min-width: 640px) {
    .pillars {
      grid-template-columns: repeat(3, 1fr);
    }
  }
  .pillar {
    padding: 20px;
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
    border-radius: var(--radius);
    background: var(--surface);
  }
  .pillar .card-icon {
    margin-bottom: 14px;
  }
  .pillar h3 {
    margin: 0;
    font-size: 1.0625rem;
  }
  .pillar p {
    margin-top: 6px;
    font-size: 0.9375rem;
    line-height: 1.55;
  }

  /* The build, step by step */
  .build {
    margin-top: 28px;
    padding: 0;
    list-style: none;
    counter-reset: step;
  }
  .build > li {
    position: relative;
    padding: 0 0 8px 56px;
    counter-increment: step;
  }
  .build > li + li {
    margin-top: 28px;
  }
  .build > li::before {
    content: counter(step);
    position: absolute;
    left: 0;
    top: -2px;
    display: grid;
    place-items: center;
    width: 36px;
    height: 36px;
    border-radius: 50%;
    background: var(--grad);
    color: #fff;
    font-weight: 700;
    font-size: 0.9375rem;
  }
  .build > li:not(:last-child)::after {
    content: '';
    position: absolute;
    left: 17px;
    top: 42px;
    bottom: -22px;
    width: 2px;
    background: var(--border);
  }
  .build h3 {
    margin-top: 2px;
    color: var(--text);
  }

  /* OEE, multiplied out */
  .equation {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 10px;
    margin: 24px 0 !important;
    padding: 18px;
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
    border-radius: var(--radius);
    background: var(--surface);
  }
  .equation .term {
    display: grid;
    gap: 2px;
    padding: 10px 14px;
    border-radius: var(--radius-sm);
    background: var(--bg);
  }
  .equation small {
    color: var(--text-2);
    font-size: 0.6875rem;
    font-weight: 600;
    letter-spacing: 0.08em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
  }
  .equation strong {
    font-size: 1.375rem;
    font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
    letter-spacing: -0.01em;
  }
  .equation .a strong {
    color: #059669;
  }
  .equation .p strong {
    color: #7c3aed;
  }
  .equation .q strong {
    color: #b45309;
  }
  .equation .result {
    background: var(--tint-strong);
  }
  .equation .result strong {
    color: var(--primary);
  }
  .equation .op {
    color: var(--text-2);
    font-size: 1.25rem;
  }
  @media (max-width: 560px) {
    .equation {
      display: grid;
      grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr;
      padding: 12px;
    }
    .equation .op {
      display: none;
    }
    .equation strong {
      font-size: 1.25rem;
    }
  }
  @media (prefers-color-scheme: dark) {
    .equation .a strong {
      color: #34d399;
    }
    .equation .p strong {
      color: #a78bfa;
    }
    .equation .q strong {
      color: #fbbf24;
    }
  }

  /* Downtime, largest first */
  .pareto ul {
    display: grid;
    gap: 10px;
    margin: 0;
    padding: 20px;
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
    border-radius: var(--radius);
    background: var(--surface);
    list-style: none;
  }
  .pareto li {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 8.5rem 1fr 3.75rem;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 12px;
    margin: 0 !important;
    font-size: 0.9375rem;
  }
  .pareto .reason {
    color: var(--text);
  }
  .pareto .bar {
    height: 10px;
    border-radius: 5px;
    background: var(--surface-2);
    overflow: hidden;
    position: relative;
  }
  .pareto .bar::before {
    content: '';
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0 auto 0 0;
    width: var(--w);
    border-radius: inherit;
    background: var(--grad);
  }
  .pareto .mins {
    color: var(--text-2);
    font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
    text-align: right;
  }
  @media (max-width: 480px) {
    .pareto li {
      grid-template-columns: 6.5rem 1fr 3.25rem;
      gap: 8px;
      font-size: 0.875rem;
    }
  }

  .next {
    margin-top: 40px !important;
  }
</style>
